Briefing: The RSVP (Relativistic Scalar–Vector Plenum) Framework

Executive Summary

The Relativistic Scalar–Vector Plenum (RSVP) is a unified field-theoretic framework developed by an entity named Flyxion. It presents a comprehensive ontology intended to coherently model cosmology, cognition, social dynamics, and semantics from a single, austere set of first principles. The core of the framework is a dynamic triplet of interacting fields: a scalar potential (Φ), a vector flow (v), and an entropy field (S). The interactions of these fields are proposed to generate all emergent structures and processes, from cosmic redshift and galaxy formation to consciousness, institutional stability, and memory.

A central and radical claim of the RSVP framework is its rejection of the metric expansion of space. Instead, it posits that phenomena like cosmic redshift are emergent effects of entropic processes within a non-expanding plenum. Structure is not a remnant of an initial event but arises from the continuous process of "gradient relaxation" where fine-grained uniformity is inherently unstable.

The theory is presented across multiple documents, including a formal academic paper, a 40-part monograph synthesizing computational "Labs," and a seven-article collection titled "The RSVP Manifold." This body of work details the RSVP stack—a hierarchical architecture comprising the base RSVP dynamics, the Entropy-Bounded Sparse Semantic Calculus (EBSSC), the Spherepop Calculus for institutional modeling, TARTAN recursive lattices for memory, and the CLIO cognitive descent algorithm for agency. The framework is designed to be rigorously falsifiable, offering a wide array of specific, testable predictions across cosmology, neuroscience, machine learning, and sociology.

1. Core Principles and Ontology

The foundational argument of the RSVP framework is a rejection of the "disciplinary partitioning of formalisms" that separates physics, cognitive science, and social theory into distinct ontologies. It aims to identify the shared dynamical principles governing structure formation at all scales. The theory is built on the premise, attributed to Alfred North Whitehead, that "The universe is not made of things, but of processes."

The (Φ, v, S) Triplet

The entire framework is built upon the interaction of three coupled fields, which are reinterpreted across different scales:

